{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\ns=dict(action='review',artifact_id='a7462b629b934bc1a7b8cf4a07f9c24c',rationale='Review fixed v1 correspondence for the local queue-release comparison, explicitly separating the internal expectation from the weaker public read-completion contract.',review_items=[dict(aspect='checker_correspondence',status='no_issue_found',source_ids=['pending-read-intent','read-api-contract','node-early-advance','doc-early-advance','node-start-and-restart','node-step-and-read','node-control-methods','node-loop','node-stop','campaign','config-activate','leader-admit','leader-match','read-pending','bootstrap-contract','storage-memory','storage-snap-append'],rationale='The accepted Claim is implementation_semantics: discharge the internal pendingReadIndexMessages condition when the first current-term commitment transition finishes. Its source is the explicit pending-read-intent statement, not an assumed public liveness guarantee or the mere absence of a helper call. read-api-contract allows lost requests/retries; that weaker external contract is preserved and does not establish a read-service violation. The tested entry is synchronous Node, whose own interface and doc.go permit Advance after persistence before application finishes, provided application batches remain ordered. The fixed driver persists Ready Entries/HardState first, defers every committed application batch at Node 2 in FIFO order, calls the public Advance, later applies the actual committed removal entry, and never assigns private protocol fields. Bootstrap, both elections, log appends and replies are actual producers in each independent execution. Node 2 is a term-3 leader with old four-voter config and commit 6/term 2 when the uniquely named ReadIndex is received. A subsequent Status call serializes behind that receive; source leader-admit queues it because the group is non-singleton and no current-term entry is committed. Only append messages from 2 to 1/4 are held. In the configuration-first case the synchronous ApplyConfChange call returns after switchToConfig changes the voter majority and maybeCommit advances to 7/term 3; no loss of leadership or change of request identity occurs. The control first releases actual buffered append messages, obtains old-config quorum commitment through the append-reply path, then applies deferred work. Both schedules terminate independently of the queue predicate. Stop is solely an ownership barrier: node-stop waits for run to close done, and node-loop returns without changing raft term, commit or pending queue; inspection after that barrier is race-free and does not create the retained request. The oracle compares independently observed term at the committed index with current term, and searches the actual pending message contexts for the independently named request. Admission and result correlate uniquely by operation_id, request, leader and unchanged leader_term; the term is intentionally constant during this selected interval. No result is a prerequisite, and no expected queue result filters applicability. Trusted execution d667238925ff4b54ac43bb5ae6f2a4f4 completed both cases: config_activation_first observes commit 7/term 3 with the original context still present and waiting_for_first_commit=true; append_reply_first observes the same commit term with empty queue and waiting=false, plus ReadState index 7. Thus one complete local violating witness and its control are present. The process passing is separate from the runner comparison, which is violated. The earlier exploration helps explain delayed read behavior but is not substituted for this formal observation.',counterevidence=[],limitations=['The obligation is the attributed internal first-commit queue-release expectation. Public ReadIndex expressly permits request loss; no unconditional public completion, safety, or linearizability violation follows.','The stopped endpoint does not prove permanent starvation; an independent later proposal can release the request, as exploratory output suggested.','The driver uses the explicit synchronous early-Advance permission and preserves application batch order. This result does not apply to asynchronous storage completion, which has different caller duties.','The check measures the committed-prefix to pending-queue handoff, not completed application of the newly committed entry or a served client read.','MemoryStorage models successful persistence without crashes; physical disk failure and recovery are outside scope.'])],feedback=dict(ref_ids=['0f9e8d1e51f64b34a017f21c8f359d94','d667238925ff4b54ac43bb5ae6f2a4f4','19745caf3e0c46ad96c930eb5730f723','node-early-advance','doc-early-advance','config-activate','campaign'],answered='The formal same-history witness shows a term-3 leader retains the original request in pendingReadIndexMessages after applying a committed voter removal advances commitment to a term-3 entry. The independently executed append-reply control clears the queue and emits its read state. The fixed-source review found correspondence for the narrow internal release expectation, under the documented synchronous early-Advance schedule. This is not a public read-completion or consensus-safety conclusion.',remaining=['The broader consequence of application bookkeeping running ahead of configuration activation remains a separate source question: which proposal/election guards consume reported applied rather than installed configuration?','No permanent starvation, physical crash-loss or unsafe-read consequence has been established.'],understanding='unchanged',rationale='Next retain the settled local behavior in the map, then inspect configuration proposal admission under the same explicit early-Advance permission. pendingConfIndex and active voter membership are separate state; comparing their consumers can determine whether this boundary is merely a read-progress issue or warrants a distinct safety discriminator. The existing storage-qualified quorum result remains independent.',question_updates={'0f9e8d1e51f64b34a017f21c8f359d94':dict(unknowns=['Whether affected deployments rely on this scheduling permission and whether any broader consequence follows are outside the local queue-release requirement.'],resume_conditions=[])}))\njson.dump(s,open('submission.json','w'),indent=2)\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-02_10-41-34-etcd_raft-real-run --submission submission.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
